  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Npc191948 and Carboxylesterase 2, mediated through the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14. This subunit is identified as a target of multiple compounds, including (1R,3R,4S,7S,9S,11S,13R,14R,15R)-4,11,13,14-tetrahydroxy-7-[(2Z,4R)-4-(hydroxymethyl)hex-2-en-2-yl]-3,9,15-trimethoxy-12,12-dimethyl-6,17-dioxabicyclo[11.3.1]heptadecan-5-one, 6-(2-hydroxypropanoyl)-4a,7-dihydro-1H-pteridine-2,4-dione, and 2,2,6-trimethyl-1-[(1E,3Z,5Z,7E,9E,11E,13Z,15Z,17E)-3,7,12,16-tetramethyl-18-(2,6,6-trimethylcyclohex-2-en-1-yl)octadeca-1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15,17-nonaenyl]-7-oxabicyclo[4.1.0]heptane. These compounds likely interact with the 26S proteasome subunit, which in turn targets Carboxylesterase 2. The scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, suggesting that the subunit may serve as a key mediator in the interaction between Npc191948 and Carboxylesterase 2. The evidence supports a mechanistic pathway where Npc191948 influences the activity of the 26S proteasome, which then affects Carboxylesterase 2. The exact nature of the interaction and the role of Npc191948 remains uncertain, but the evidence collectively points to a plausible biological connection.",
